---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import ImmuneSim from '../../simulators/immune/ImmuneSim.astro';
import { immuneQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/immune';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What the immune system does' },
  { id: 'organs', label: 'The immune organs' },
  { id: 'barriers', label: 'First line: barriers' },
  { id: 'innate', label: 'Innate immunity' },
  { id: 'adaptive', label: 'Adaptive immunity' },
  { id: 'memory', label: 'Immune memory and vaccines' },
  { id: 'relevance', label: 'The immune system and health' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Chapter 21: The Lymphatic and Immune System. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-introduction' },
  { text: 'Murphy, K., & Weaver, C. (2016). Janeway’s Immunobiology (9th ed.). Garland Science.' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 42: The Immune System. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-introduction' },
  { text: 'Perelson, A. S. (2002). Modelling viral and immune system dynamics. Nature Reviews Immunology, 2(1), 28–36.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="anatomy-physiology"
  topic="immune-system"
  description="Interactive immune system simulator: explore the immune organs, introduce a pathogen and watch innate and adaptive immunity respond, then compare primary and secondary immune responses."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={immuneQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every day your body meets bacteria, viruses and other microbes. Most never get past your skin, and the few that do
      are usually destroyed before you notice. Explore the organs of the immune system, let a pathogen in through a cut,
      and watch the body’s defences respond, first quickly and generally, then slowly but precisely, and faster still the
      next time.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <ImmuneSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Immune organs:</strong> click the bone marrow, thymus, spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils or vessels to read what each does. Press <strong>Start</strong> to see white blood cells moving in the blood and lymph.</li>
      <li><strong>Fight an infection:</strong> choose a pathogen and dose, then press <strong>Introduce pathogen</strong>. Watch inflammation and phagocytes act within hours, then B cells, T cells and antibodies take over. Follow the graph below.</li>
      <li>When the infection clears, the person has memory cells. Press <strong>Introduce pathogen</strong> again and compare how quickly it is cleared.</li>
      <li><strong>Primary vs secondary:</strong> see both responses side by side and read how much faster and larger the second one is.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Educational model only.</strong> This simulator is a simplified teaching model. It cannot diagnose,
      treat or predict any real infection, and its numbers do not describe how any real person will respond. If you are
      worried about an infection or your immunity, speak to a qualified healthcare professional.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what">What the immune system does</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>immune system</strong> defends the body against <strong>pathogens</strong>, the microorganisms that
    cause disease, such as bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites. It also removes damaged and abnormal cells. Unlike the
    heart or lungs, it is not one organ but a network of barriers, organs, cells and chemicals spread through the whole
    body. Its defences work in layers: <strong>barriers</strong> keep pathogens out, <strong>innate immunity</strong>
    responds quickly to anything foreign, and <strong>adaptive immunity</strong> builds a targeted response and remembers
    it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="organs">The immune organs</h2>
  <p>
    All white blood cells are made in the <strong>bone marrow</strong>, the soft tissue inside bones such as the femur
    and sternum. B cells mature there, while T cells move to the <strong>thymus</strong> in the chest, where they learn to
    ignore the body’s own cells. The <strong>lymphatic system</strong> collects fluid that leaks out of capillaries into
    tissues. This fluid, called <strong>lymph</strong>, flows through <strong>lymphatic vessels</strong> and passes through
    hundreds of <strong>lymph nodes</strong>, small filters packed with B and T cells, before returning to the blood near
    the heart. The <strong>spleen</strong> filters the blood in the same way, and the <strong>tonsils</strong> guard the
    throat. Swollen lymph nodes in the neck during a cold are a sign that lymphocytes there are multiplying.
  </p>

  <h2 id="barriers">First line: barriers</h2>
  <p>
    Most pathogens never get inside. Unbroken <strong>skin</strong> is a tough, dry barrier. The airways are lined with
    <strong>mucus</strong> that traps microbes and <strong>cilia</strong> that sweep it away. <strong>Stomach acid</strong>
    kills many swallowed microbes, and <strong>tears</strong> and saliva contain enzymes that break down bacteria. A cut
    or burn breaks this barrier, which is why the simulator introduces its pathogen through a wound.
  </p>

  <h2 id="innate">Innate immunity</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>innate</strong> response starts within minutes and is the same for every pathogen. Damaged cells release
    chemicals such as histamine, causing <strong>inflammation</strong>: nearby capillaries widen and become leakier, so
    the area becomes red, swollen, warm and sometimes painful. White blood cells called <strong>phagocytes</strong>
    (neutrophils and macrophages) squeeze out of the capillaries, move towards the pathogens and
    <strong>engulf</strong> them in a process called <strong>phagocytosis</strong>, destroying them with enzymes. Chemicals
    released during infection can also reset the brain’s thermostat, causing a <strong>fever</strong>. Innate immunity
    often clears small infections alone; if not, it holds the pathogen back while the adaptive response prepares.
  </p>

  <h2 id="adaptive">Adaptive immunity</h2>
  <p>
    Every pathogen carries <strong>antigens</strong>, molecules that the immune system recognises as foreign. Phagocytes
    carry pieces of these antigens to the lymph nodes and present them to <strong>lymphocytes</strong>. Each B or T cell
    has receptors that fit just one antigen, so at first only a few cells can respond. When a matching cell is found it
    divides again and again, a process called <strong>clonal expansion</strong>, which is why this response takes several
    days the first time.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>B cells</strong> become <strong>plasma cells</strong> that release <strong>antibodies</strong>, Y-shaped proteins that stick to antigens. Antibodies clump pathogens together, neutralise toxins and mark pathogens for phagocytes.</li>
    <li><strong>Helper T cells</strong> coordinate the response and activate B cells.</li>
    <li><strong>Cytotoxic (killer) T cells</strong> destroy body cells that have been infected by viruses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="memory">Immune memory and vaccines</h2>
  <p>
    After an infection, most of the expanded B and T cells die, but some remain as long-lived <strong>memory
    cells</strong>. If the same pathogen returns, they recognise it straight away. The <strong>secondary
    response</strong> starts sooner, produces far more antibodies and usually clears the pathogen before it can cause
    illness. This is what being <strong>immune</strong> means. <strong>Vaccines</strong> use this: they contain a harmless
    form of an antigen, such as an inactivated pathogen or a piece of its protein, so the body makes memory cells without
    having the disease. When many people are vaccinated, a pathogen struggles to spread, which also protects people who
    cannot be vaccinated; this is called <strong>herd immunity</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="relevance">The immune system and health</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes the immune system responds when it should not. In <strong>allergies</strong> it reacts to harmless
    substances such as pollen; in <strong>autoimmune diseases</strong>, such as type 1 diabetes, it attacks the body’s
    own cells. In <strong>immunodeficiency</strong>, for example in HIV infection, it is weakened and infections become
    more dangerous. Antibiotics kill bacteria but have no effect on viruses, and their overuse helps antibiotic
    resistance evolve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Pathogen</dt>
    <dd>A microorganism that causes disease.</dd>
    <dt>Antigen</dt>
    <dd>A molecule recognised by the immune system as foreign.</dd>
    <dt>Antibody</dt>
    <dd>A Y-shaped protein made by plasma B cells that binds to a specific antigen.</dd>
    <dt>Phagocytosis</dt>
    <dd>The engulfing and digestion of a pathogen by a phagocyte.</dd>
    <dt>Lymphocyte</dt>
    <dd>A B or T cell, the white blood cells of adaptive immunity.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Antibodies stay high for life after an infection.”</strong> Antibody levels fall; it is memory cells that last.</li>
    <li><strong>“Antibiotics cure colds.”</strong> Colds are caused by viruses, and antibiotics only work against bacteria.</li>
    <li><strong>“A fever is always harmful.”</strong> A moderate fever is part of the body’s defence, although very high fevers need medical attention.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Barriers stop most pathogens; innate immunity responds quickly with inflammation and phagocytes.</li>
    <li>Adaptive immunity uses B cells, antibodies and T cells that target one specific antigen.</li>
    <li>White blood cells are made in the bone marrow; T cells mature in the thymus; lymph nodes and the spleen filter lymph and blood.</li>
    <li>Memory cells make the secondary response faster and larger, which is how vaccines work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Model note: pathogen numbers, cell numbers and antibody levels are in relative units from a small set of equations
    tuned to show textbook-shaped curves. Real responses involve many more cell types and signals and vary greatly between
    pathogens and people. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">educational and medical disclaimer</a>.
  </p>
</SimulatorLayout>
